.\" mutos_as.1 - manual page for the MUTOS 1700 cross-assembler
.\" Formatted with the standard man(7) macro set (.TH/.SH/.TP/...).
.\" View with: man ./mutos_as.1   or   groff -man -Tascii mutos_as.1 | less
.TH MUTOS_AS 1 "September 2026" "MUTOS 1700" "MUTOS 1700 Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
mutos_as \- MUTOS 1700 cross-assembler for the 8086/80186
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mutos_as
.RB [ \-W ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RI [ file " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mutos_as
is a two-pass cross-assembler that translates MUTOS 1700 assembly
language source into a relocatable
.BR a.out (5)-format
object file for the Intel 8086/80186 (NEC V30-compatible)
instruction set, as used by the MUTOS 1700 embedded system.
Its output is intended to be processed by
.BR mutos_ld (1)
to produce a linked, loadable program, exactly like the object files
produced by the original hardware toolchain it reproduces.
.PP
Source is read from the concatenation of all
.I file
operands, in the order given, as if the files had been joined with
.BR cat (1)
before assembly; a missing newline at the end of one file is silently
supplied so that a statement never straddles a file boundary purely
by accident.
If no
.I file
operand is given, source is read from the standard input instead.
.PP
Assembly proceeds in two passes over the source. Pass 1 walks every
statement to compute instruction sizes and assign an address to every
label; Pass 2 walks the source again, now with a complete symbol
table, to emit the final instruction bytes and relocation entries.
Errors detected in Pass 1 abort assembly before Pass 2 is attempted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
Write the assembled object file to
.I output
instead of the default
.BR a.out .
.TP
.B \-W
Suppress all diagnostic messages that would otherwise be printed to
standard error. Errors are still detected and still prevent a
successful assembly (see
.B DIAGNOSTICS
below); only the printed text is affected.
.PP
If assembly completes with no errors and no symbol remains
referenced but undefined, the output file is marked executable
(mode 0755); otherwise it is left non-executable (mode 0644) and
must be resolved by
.BR mutos_ld (1)
before it can be run.
.PP
.B asf
is recognized as an alternate name for this program (via
.IR argv [0]),
matching the historical convention of a separate assembler name for
source containing K1810WM87 (8087-compatible) arithmetic-coprocessor
instructions. No concrete coprocessor instruction mnemonics are
currently implemented, so invoking the program as
.B asf
currently behaves identically to invoking it as
.BR mutos_as .
.SH "ASSEMBLY LANGUAGE"
.SS Numbers
Numeric literals are decimal by default.
A trailing period
.RI ( "e.g." " 128." )
is accepted as a stylistic decimal terminator and has no effect on
the value.
A leading slash introduces a hexadecimal literal
.RI ( "e.g." " /FF" ", /2A0" ).
There is no octal literal syntax.
.SS Registers
The eight 16-bit general registers
.RB ( ax ", " bx ", " cx ", " dx ", " si ", " di ", " bp ", " sp ),
their 8-bit halves where applicable
.RB ( al ", " ah ", " bl ", " bh ", " cl ", " ch ", " dl ", " dh ),
and the four segment registers
.RB ( cs ", " ds ", " es ", " ss )
are recognized by name.
.SS "Operand size markers"
An asterisk
.RB ( * )
prefix marks an operand as byte-sized; a hash
.RB ( # )
prefix marks it as word-sized. Both characters also function as
ordinary infix operators (multiplication and, contextually,
immediate-value grouping) when not used as a leading size marker.
For an
.B immediate
operand the marker is honored literally, regardless of the value's
actual magnitude.
For a
.B displacement
in an indirect memory operand, the marker has no effect: the choice
between an 8-bit and a 16-bit displacement encoding is made from the
resolved value alone, except that a displacement expression which
references any symbol always uses the 16-bit form, since the
symbol's final value cannot be assumed to keep fitting in a signed
byte once linked.
Whether an instruction operates on a byte or a word is otherwise
selected by the mnemonic: a
.B b
suffix
.RI ( "e.g." " movb" ", " cmpb ", " addb )
selects the byte form; the unsuffixed mnemonic
.RI ( "e.g." " mov" ", " cmp ", " add )
selects the word form, or infers byte size from an explicit
byte-class register operand where the instruction supports that
inference (e.g.\& plain
.BR "mov al,*0" ", " "mov al,_x" " or " "mov _x,al"
still assemble to byte-sized moves).
.SS "Addressing modes"
.TP
.I register
A bare register name, e.g.\&
.BR ax ", " bl .
.TP
.IR disp "(" reg )
Register-indirect memory access, optionally with a leading
displacement expression, e.g.\&
.BR (si) ", " 4.(bx) ", " \-8.(bp) ", " 174.+_extern(bx) .
.TP
.I symbol
A bare symbolic or absolute address (direct addressing), e.g.\&
.BR _global_ .
As the source operand of
.B lea
it yields that address itself, e.g.\&
.B lea ax,L10000
(a relocated 16-bit address field, like any direct operand).
.TP
.I immediate
An immediate value or address-of expression, typically introduced by
a
.B *
or
.B #
size marker, e.g.\&
.BR *4. ", " #_table .
.TP
.BI @ reg
Register-indirect jump/call/branch target (mod=11 addressing) \(em
distinct from the data-indirect
.RI ( reg )
form above \(em used only as the operand of
.BR jmp ", " br ", " call ", " calli ", and " jmpi .
.TP
.BI @ disp ( reg )
Memory-indirect jump/call/branch target: the word stored at
.IR disp + reg
is the real destination, e.g.\& a dispatch-table lookup such as
.BR "call @8.+_cdevsw(bx)" .
.PP
The bare period
.RB ( . )
denotes the current location counter.
A single digit followed by a colon defines a numeric local label
.RI ( "e.g." " 1:" ),
referenced elsewhere as
.I N\c
.B f
(nearest following definition) or
.I N\c
.B b
(nearest preceding definition).
.SS Segments
Three segments are available:
.BR .text ,
.BR .data ,
and
.BR .bss ,
selected by a directive of the same name on its own line; the
current segment determines both where subsequent bytes are emitted
and which location counter subsequent labels are defined against.
.B .bss
reserves zero-filled space only \(em it has no byte stream of its
own \(em typically populated with
.B .blkb
(see below).
.SS Directives
.TP
.BI .globl\  symbol
Marks
.I symbol
as externally visible.
.TP
.BI .comm\  symbol , size
Declares an uninitialized external symbol of
.I size
bytes. Unlike
.BR .bss ,
this does not reserve space in the current module directly; the
symbol is left undefined with
.I size
recorded for the linker to allocate, exactly like a C
tentative-definition global.
.TP
.BI .blkb\  count
Reserves
.I count
zero-filled bytes at the current location in the current segment
(normally
.BR .bss ).
.TP
.BI .word\  expr\  "[, " expr " ...]"
Emits one 16-bit value per comma-separated expression into the
current segment.
.TP
.BI .byte\  expr\  "[, " expr " ...]"
Emits one 8-bit value per comma-separated expression into the
current segment.
.PP
A bare expression on its own statement line, with no directive or
mnemonic at all, is likewise treated as an implicit
.B .word
\(em commonly used to build a table of label addresses, e.g.\&
.PP
.RS
.nf
.I jmptab :
L10003
L10004
L10005
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI .asciz\  *string*
Emits
.I string
followed by a terminating NUL byte. The string is delimited by
matching asterisks rather than double quotes.
.TP
.B .even
Pads the current segment's location counter to the next even
address with a single zero byte, if it is currently odd.
.TP
.IR name " = " value
Defines
.I name
as an absolute constant equal to
.IR value ,
resolved at assembly time.
.TP
\fB.\fR=\fB.\fR+\fIN\fR
Advances the current location counter by
.I N
bytes, zero-filling the reserved span in the output.
.TP
.BI .float\  number\  "[, " number " ...]"
Emits one 4-byte floating-point constant per comma-separated
.IR number ,
written as
.BR atof (3)
reads it (an optional sign, digits with an optional decimal point,
an optional
.B e
exponent), optionally prefixed with
.BR 0f ,
e.g.\&
.BR "L10000: .float 3.50000000000000000e+00" ,
the form the compiler writes for every floating constant.
The constant is in the MUTOS floating format: little-endian, the
excess-128 binary exponent in the highest byte, the sign in the top
bit of the byte below it, then the mantissa bits after its leading
one.
Only a value exactly representable in the format's 24 significant
bits is accepted, and encoded exactly; a value that is not is
rejected with an error, since how the original assembler rounds such
a value is not confirmed.
No alignment is implied and no relocation is ever needed.
.IP
Zero is not stored as zero bytes: the original assembler writes
.B bc a2 31 00
(exponent byte 0 with a leftover mantissa).
Those bytes are confirmed only for the spelling the compiler writes,
.BR 0.00000000000000000e+00 ,
so exactly that shape (no minus sign, 17 digits after the point,
exponent 0) is accepted; any other spelling of zero is rejected with
an error.
.TP
.BI .double\  number\  "[, " number " ...]"
Like
.BR .float ,
but emits one 8-byte constant per
.IR number :
the same format with 56 significant bits (55 stored after the
leading one), e.g.\&
.B .double 1.00000000023283064365386962890625
gives
.BR "00 00 80 00 00 00 00 81" .
The number is written as for
.B .float
without a prefix, or with the manual's
.B 0d
prefix, after which the exponent is marked with
.B d
instead of
.BR e ,
e.g.\&
.BR "0d1.5d3" .
Only exactly representable values are accepted, and every zero is
rejected: no bytes of the original assembler for a zero
.B .double
are confirmed.
.PP
Other directives this assembler does not implement are ignored.
.SS "Branches and jumps"
The real conditional jump mnemonics
.RI ( "e.g." " je" ", " jne ", " jb ", " jae ", " jg ", " jle ,
and their many synonyms such as
.BR jz / jnz ", " jc / jnc ", " jnae / jnb ", " jng / jnge )
always assemble to the short, 2-byte relative form.
A bare
.B j
.I label
likewise always assembles short.
.B jmp
.I label
always assembles to the long, 3-byte relative form, with no
size-based selection between the two.
.PP
The pseudo-branch mnemonics
.RB ( beq ", " bne ", " blt ", " bge ", " ble ", " bgt ", " bhi ", " bhis ,
.BR blo ", " blos ", and their Anlage-B-documented synonym " bloss )
unconditionally expand to a 5-byte sequence (a negated short
conditional jump around a following long jump), regardless of how
close the target actually is.
.B br
.I label
is an unconditional long branch and is a plain synonym for
.B jmp
.IR label .
.SS "Prefixes"
.B rep
(and its synonyms
.BR repe / repz ", " repne / repnz )
and
.BI seg\  sr
are each written as a one-line pseudo-instruction immediately
preceding the instruction they modify, rather than as a token
attached to that instruction.
.SH "OUTPUT FORMAT"
The object file written is in the MUTOS a.out relocatable format
(see
.BR a.out (5)):
a fixed header, the text segment, the data segment, a text
relocation table, a data relocation table, and a symbol table, in
that order.
The text and data segments are each rounded up to an even size with
one zero byte at the end of the assembly; switching between
.B .text
and
.B .data
in the middle of the source inserts nothing, so a constant's
.BR .data " ... " .text
block written between two instructions leaves the code contiguous.
The symbol table is written in
.I creation order
\(em the order in which each distinct symbol name is first
mentioned, whether as a label definition or as an operand reference,
during the left-to-right scan of the source \(em which is also the
order used to number symbols for the
.B R_EXT
field of external relocation entries.
Both PC-relative relocations (an external
.BR call / jmp / branch
target) and absolute relocations (an external, text-, data-, or
bss-segment symbol's address embedded in an immediate or a memory
displacement) are emitted as required; a same-segment address
difference that is provably location-independent
.RI ( "e.g." " a\-b"
where
.I a
and
.I b
are both local text labels) requires no relocation entry and
receives none.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Diagnostic messages are printed to standard error in the form
.RS
.B "error: "
.I message
.B " at line "
.I N
.RE
and are intended to be self-explanatory. All errors detected during
Pass 1 are reported together, and Pass 2 is not attempted if any
were found. The program's exit status is 0 on success and 1 if any
error was detected, a required file could not be opened, or the
output file could not be written \(em regardless of whether
.B \-W
suppressed the corresponding message text.
.SH LIMITATIONS
The direct (segment:offset literal) operand form of
.B calli
and
.B jmpi
is not implemented; only their indirect
(\fB@\fIreg\fR
and
\fB@\fIdisp\fB(\fIreg\fB)\fR)
forms are available. No concrete K1810WM87/8087 floating-point
coprocessor instruction mnemonics are implemented (see
.B asf
above).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I a.out
Default output object file.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mutos_ld (1),
.BR a.out (5)
